# {{name}}

{{#if web}}
An HTTP service on [Gióng](https://giong.run), the thread-per-core async runtime for Rust.
{{/if}}
{{#if grpc}}
A gRPC service on [Gióng](https://giong.run), the thread-per-core async runtime for Rust.
{{/if}}
{{#if worker}}
A background worker on [Gióng](https://giong.run), the thread-per-core async runtime for Rust.
{{/if}}
Made with `giong new` from giong {{giong_version}}.

## Work on it

```sh
{{#if db}}
giong db up              # starts the database in Docker and writes its URL into .env
{{/if}}
{{#if dev_background}}
giong dev --background   # builds and starts the service, then rebuilds it on every change
giong dev status         # is it running, and the last build's errors (giong dev logs: its output)
{{/if}}
{{#if !dev_background}}
giong dev                # builds, runs, and restarts on every change
{{/if}}
giong check              # compiler, clippy and giong's own checks, as one list
giong test               # the tests, on io_uring and on epoll
{{#if dev_background}}
giong dev stop           # when you are done
{{/if}}
```
{{#if dev_background}}

One `giong dev` per project, started once: edit the code and it swaps in the new build
{{#if worker}}
by itself.
{{#else}}
without refusing a connection. Try the service on the address it prints. A change that
does not compile or start stops it, so nothing old answers: `giong dev status` says why.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}

{{#if dev_background}}
Without the `giong` command (in CI, say), `cargo test` works too: the tests read `.env`
themselves.
{{#else}}
Without the `giong` command, plain cargo works too: `cargo run`, `cargo test`
(the tests read `.env` themselves).
{{/if}}

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
{{#if web}}
| `src/main.rs` | reads the configuration, starts one worker per core |
| `src/lib.rs` | the app: routes and middleware, built once per worker |
{{/if}}
{{#if web & sql}}
| `src/users.rs` | `/users` |
{{/if}}
{{#if web & redis & !sql}}
| `src/counters.rs` | `/counters` |
{{/if}}
{{#if web & !db}}
| `src/hello.rs` | `/hello` and `/echo` |
{{/if}}
{{#if grpc}}
| `proto/service.proto` | the service, compiled at build time by `build.rs` (no `protoc`) |
| `src/main.rs` | starts one gRPC server per core |
| `src/lib.rs` | the service: one method per rpc |
{{/if}}
{{#if worker}}
| `src/main.rs` | reads the configuration, starts one worker per core |
| `src/lib.rs` | the jobs and the loop that runs them |
{{/if}}
{{#if sql}}
| `migrations/` | SQL applied in order by `giong db migrate` |
{{/if}}
| `tests/` | the service end to end |
| `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.cursor/`, `.claude/` | what coding agents need to know (`giong ai init`) |

## Configuration

Everything comes from the environment; `.env.example` lists the variables. In
development `giong dev` and the tests read `.env`; in production nothing reads it.
{{#if !worker}}
Without `LISTEN` the service listens on `127.0.0.1` only; the Dockerfile sets
`LISTEN=0.0.0.0:{{default_port}}`, and a deployment without Docker sets it too.
{{/if}}

## Deploy

```sh
docker build -t {{name}} .
```

Docker's default seccomp profile blocks io_uring: the service then runs on giong's
epoll driver and says so once on stderr. To run on io_uring, allow its three system
calls:

```sh
curl -fsSLo docker-default.json \
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/moby/profiles/main/seccomp/default.json
jq '.syscalls += [{"names": ["io_uring_setup", "io_uring_enter", "io_uring_register"],
                   "action": "SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"}]' docker-default.json > giong-seccomp.json
{{#if worker}}
docker run --security-opt seccomp=giong-seccomp.json --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 {{name}}
{{#else}}
docker run --security-opt seccomp=giong-seccomp.json --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \
  -p {{default_port}}:{{default_port}} {{name}}
{{/if}}
```

More in giong's [deployment notes](https://github.com/giong-run/Giong/blob/v{{giong_version}}/docs/DEPLOYMENT.md).
